          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How is blood pressure actually measured, and why is more than one reading needed?",
          "answer": "**How it is measured:** The sources I searched do not describe the cuff itself, how it is inflated or deflated, or how the two numbers are picked out. I can't explain those steps from them. A clinic nurse or pharmacist can walk you through the procedure.\n\n**What the sources do say about the two numbers:** Blood pressure is written as two numbers, both in millimeters of mercury. The top number (systolic) is the pressure when the heart contracts. The bottom number (diastolic) is the pressure between beats. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*, general clinical reference.)\n\n**Why more than one reading is needed:**\n- Pressure rises and falls through the day with activity, stress, pain, caffeine, sleep and time of day. It is usually lowest during sleep. A single reading captures one moment, so one high reading is not a diagnosis. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*.)\n- Under US guidance, a diagnosis is based on an average of two or more readings taken on two or more separate occasions. (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*.)\n- Readings taken outside the clinic, such as at home or over 24 hours with a portable monitor, can show two patterns that office readings alone may miss. In white-coat hypertension, office readings are raised but readings outside the office are not. In masked hypertension, the reverse is true, and the risk can be underestimated. (Same source.)\n\n**Limits of this answer:** The retrieved text also includes a fragment saying that some technique factors can push a reading up by about the width of a whole blood pressure stage. The passage was cut off, so I can't tell you which factors it means. Ask a clinician about how readings are taken where you are measured.\n\nIf you have a reading that worries you, or you feel unwell with symptoms such as chest pain, severe headache, weakness, trouble speaking or shortness of breath, seek urgent medical care now rather than waiting for more readings.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
